1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,659
This is STAT 331, video 20. It covers Lecture 10: interactions and non-linear terms.

2
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:26,093
After this video, you can write a model in which each group has its own intercept and its own slope. You can interpret an interaction coefficient. You can fit and interpret quadratic terms. And you can solve the exercise and the three practice questions of Lecture 10.

3
00:00:26,543 --> 00:00:42,073
First, a short recap of Lecture 9. The data are the fishermen mercury data. MeHg is the concentration of methyl mercury in a hair sample. The colour shows fishpart, the parts of fish that a person eats.

4
00:00:42,523 --> 00:00:58,331
In Lecture 9, we put fishpart into the model with indicator variables. An indicator variable is 1 when a condition is true, and 0 when it is false. The fitted model gives four parallel lines, one for each group.

5
00:00:58,781 --> 00:01:15,976
To test a joint null hypothesis, beta star equals 0, we use the F test. q is the length of beta star. Under the null hypothesis, F has an F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

6
00:01:16,426 --> 00:01:31,189
Use a t test for one coefficient, or for one linear combination of coefficients. Use an F test for a joint null hypothesis about two or more coefficients. This video uses both tests again.

7
00:01:31,639 --> 00:01:46,252
Slide 9 writes the same model with shorter names. M i is the indicator that subject i eats muscle tissue only. MW i and W i are the indicators of the other two eating groups.

8
00:01:46,702 --> 00:02:00,547
The table shows the three indicators for each group. For group N, all three indicators are 0. N is the baseline category, also called the referent category. Its mean has no extra beta.

9
00:02:00,997 --> 00:02:11,707
Put the indicator values into the model. Each group gets its own intercept. For example, group M has the intercept beta 0 plus beta M.

10
00:02:12,157 --> 00:02:26,365
But all four lines have the same slope, beta 1. So the model says: one more kilogram of weight changes the mean MeHg by the same amount in every group. This is the common slope model.

11
00:02:26,815 --> 00:02:43,391
Now we let each group have its own slope. Slide 11 gives the model. Look at its design matrix X. Each row is one subject. Here is one subject from each group. The first five columns are the columns of the common slope model.

12
00:02:43,841 --> 00:02:57,963
Now add three product columns. Each one is weight i times one indicator. A product of two covariates in a model is an interaction term. A term with one covariate alone is a main effect.

13
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:13,517
A product column equals the weight when the subject is in that group. In all other rows, it is 0. For example, the column weight i times M i is 73 for the subject in group M, and 0 for the others.

14
00:03:13,967 --> 00:03:28,005
Each column has its own coefficient. beta 1 M, beta 1 M W, and beta 1 W are the coefficients of the interaction terms. The model has eight coefficients in total.

15
00:03:28,455 --> 00:03:35,196
What is the mean when N i is 1, and when MW i is 1? Pause the video and try it.

16
00:03:35,646 --> 00:03:50,729
For group N, all three indicators are 0. So all three product terms are also 0. The mean is beta 0 plus beta 1 times weight. The black line shows it. The picture uses made-up numbers.

17
00:03:51,179 --> 00:04:04,747
For group MW, MW i is 1, and the other two indicators are 0. Two extra terms stay in the model: beta M W, and beta 1 M W times weight.

18
00:04:05,197 --> 00:04:20,727
Group the terms. The intercept is beta 0 plus beta M W. The slope is beta 1 plus beta 1 M W. So group MW has its own intercept and its own slope. The teal line shows it.

19
00:04:21,177 --> 00:04:36,815
Slide 15 asks: what is beta 1, and what is beta 1 M W. Take group N. Compare weight a plus 1 with weight a. The difference of the two means is beta 1. So beta 1 is the slope of the N line.

20
00:04:37,265 --> 00:04:48,123
Do the same for group MW. The difference of the two means is beta 1 plus beta 1 M W. This is the slope of the MW line.

21
00:04:48,573 --> 00:05:03,251
Subtract the two slopes. beta 1 M W is the change for MW, minus the change for N. Each change is a difference of two means. So beta 1 M W is a difference in differences.

22
00:05:03,701 --> 00:05:20,853
In words: beta 1 M W is how much larger the change in mean MeHg per kilogram of weight is in group MW than in the baseline group N. If beta 1 M W is 0, the two lines are parallel.

23
00:05:21,303 --> 00:05:26,999
The lecture R code fits both models to the mercury data. These are the data again.

24
00:05:27,449 --> 00:05:30,969
The common slope model gives four parallel lines.

25
00:05:31,419 --> 00:05:46,416
The interaction model gives each group its own slope, so the lines are not parallel. The table gives each fitted slope, computed from the data. Each slope is beta 1 hat plus the interaction coefficient of the group.

26
00:05:46,866 --> 00:05:58,599
Group N has only 10 subjects, and group W has 9. So the slopes of these two lines are uncertain. Look at the group sizes before you interpret a slope.

27
00:05:59,049 --> 00:06:16,436
The next example uses the real estate data. Each row is one house that was sold. SalePrice is the price in thousands of dollars. SqrFeet is the floor area in square feet. The table shows the first six houses from slide 16.

28
00:06:16,886 --> 00:06:24,417
Air is an indicator variable. It is 1 if the house has air conditioning, A C. It is 0 if not.

29
00:06:24,867 --> 00:06:38,797
The plot shows all 521 houses. Blue points have air conditioning, and black points do not. 433 houses have air conditioning. Larger houses have higher prices.

30
00:06:39,247 --> 00:06:47,013
We now compare three models. They differ in how the line for A C houses can differ from the line for the other houses.

31
00:06:47,463 --> 00:07:02,012
Model M1 has SqrFeet and Air. The two lines have a common slope, but different intercepts. The lines are parallel. The lines in these panels are fitted to the data, and drawn from 0 square feet.

32
00:07:02,462 --> 00:07:14,430
Model M2 has SqrFeet and the product SqrFeet times Air, but not Air alone. The two lines have a common intercept, but different slopes. They start at the same point.

33
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:28,085
Model M3 has Air, SqrFeet, and their product. The two lines have different intercepts and different slopes. In R, Air star SqrFeet is a short way to ask for these three terms.

34
00:07:28,535 --> 00:07:42,231
M3 has both main effects and the interaction. M2 leaves out the main effect of Air. Later in this video, the hierarchical principle says why M3 is usually the better choice.

35
00:07:42,681 --> 00:07:50,255
Slide 17 shows the data with the fitted lines of M1 and M3. We drew it again from the data.

36
00:07:50,705 --> 00:08:00,006
The solid lines are M1. They are parallel. The A C line is higher by 26.6 thousand dollars at every floor area.

37
00:08:00,456 --> 00:08:07,603
The dotted lines are M3. Each group has its own slope. The numbers are computed from the data.

38
00:08:08,053 --> 00:08:18,357
In M3, the A C line is steeper. For small houses, the two lines are close. For large houses, A C houses have a much higher mean price.

39
00:08:18,807 --> 00:08:36,001
Now interpret each coefficient of M3. beta 0 hat is minus 3.22. It is the mean price of a house without A C, and with 0 square feet. No house has 0 square feet. So this number has no practical meaning.

40
00:08:36,451 --> 00:08:51,129
beta 1 hat, the coefficient of Air, is minus 78.87. It is the difference in mean price, A C minus no A C, at 0 square feet. Alone, it also has no practical meaning.

41
00:08:51,579 --> 00:09:09,669
beta 2 hat is 0.1049. For houses without A C, the mean price goes up by 0.1049 thousand dollars per square foot. Over 1000 square feet, that is 104.9 thousand dollars.

42
00:09:10,119 --> 00:09:28,487
beta 3 hat, the interaction coefficient, is 0.0559. The A C slope is larger by this amount. The A C slope is 0.1608, or 160.8 thousand dollars per 1000 square feet.

43
00:09:28,937 --> 00:09:45,812
With an interaction, the difference between the groups depends on the floor area. At 2000 square feet, it is minus 78.87, plus 0.0559 times 2000. That is 32.9 thousand dollars.

44
00:09:46,262 --> 00:10:04,779
This exercise is on slides 18 and 19. 59 students rated the course. I of Fast i is 1 if the student finds the pace fast. I of Grad i is 1 if the student considers grad school. The model has both indicators and their product.

45
00:10:05,229 --> 00:10:23,533
The slide gives the R output. coef gives the four estimates. vcov gives the estimated variance matrix of beta hat: sigma hat squared, times X transpose X inverse. The degrees of freedom are 59 minus 4, which is 55.

46
00:10:23,983 --> 00:10:39,919
Put the 0 and 1 values into the model. Each cell of the table has its own mean. For example, a student with both indicators equal to 1 has the mean beta 0, plus beta 1, plus beta 2, plus beta 3.

47
00:10:40,369 --> 00:10:47,687
Question 1. Is the mean course rating associated with perceived lecture pace? Pause the video and try it.

48
00:10:48,137 --> 00:11:03,582
Compare the two cells in each row. For students who do not consider grad school, a fast pace changes the mean by beta 1. For students who consider grad school, it changes the mean by beta 1 plus beta 3.

49
00:11:04,032 --> 00:11:25,109
Pace has no association with the mean rating when both changes are 0. This is true exactly when beta 1 is 0 and beta 3 is 0. So the null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 3 equals 0. This is a joint null, so we use the F test with q equal to 2.

50
00:11:25,559 --> 00:11:43,757
beta star hat is the vector of beta 1 hat and beta 3 hat. The vcov output already includes sigma hat squared. So F is beta star hat transpose, times the inverse of the 2 by 2 block of vcov, times beta star hat, over q.

51
00:11:44,207 --> 00:12:09,209
Invert the 2 by 2 block. Swap the two diagonal entries, change the sign of the other two, and divide by the determinant, 0.008411. Then multiply out. The quadratic form is 0.1167 over 0.008411. Divide by q, which is 2. F is 6.937.

52
00:12:09,659 --> 00:12:33,019
The 95 percent quantile of F with 2 and 55 degrees of freedom is 3.165. The observed F is larger. The p-value is the upper tail area, 0.0021. So we reject the null hypothesis. The data give evidence that the mean rating is associated with lecture pace.

53
00:12:33,469 --> 00:12:53,672
Question 2. Test for association between pace and rating among students who consider grad school. Pause the video and try it. For these students, Grad is 1, so the pace effect is beta 1 plus beta 3. The null hypothesis is beta 1 plus beta 3 equals 0.

54
00:12:54,122 --> 00:13:12,341
This is one linear combination of coefficients, so we use a t test. Its variance is the variance of beta 1 hat, plus the variance of beta 3 hat, plus 2 times their covariance. The green and orange cells of vcov give these numbers.

55
00:13:12,791 --> 00:13:31,799
The variance is 0.0907, plus 0.1834, minus 2 times 0.0907. That is 0.0927. The standard error is its square root, 0.3045.

56
00:13:32,249 --> 00:14:12,371
The estimate is minus 0.4709 minus 0.5585, which is minus 1.0294. t is minus 1.0294 over 0.3045, which is minus 3.381. Its absolute value is larger than 2.004, the t quantile with 55 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 0.0013. We reject the null hypothesis. In this group, students who find the pace fast give a lower mean rating.

57
00:14:12,821 --> 00:14:28,437
Question 3. Now take the students who do not consider grad school. Pause the video and try it. For them, Grad is 0, so the pace effect is beta 1 alone. The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals 0.

58
00:14:28,887 --> 00:14:47,724
The standard error is the square root of 0.0907, which is 0.3012. t is minus 0.4709 over 0.3012, which is minus 1.563.

59
00:14:48,174 --> 00:15:05,134
The absolute value of t is smaller than 2.004. The p-value is 0.124. We do not reject the null hypothesis. The data do not show an association between pace and rating in this group.

60
00:15:05,584 --> 00:15:15,056
The answers to questions 2 and 3 are different. So the effect of pace depends on grad school interest. This is what an interaction means.

61
00:15:15,506 --> 00:15:30,631
Items 4 and 5 ask for the mean rating of students in the morning or the afternoon section. The model has no section variable. So we cannot answer them as written. We show the same method for the four cells of the model.

62
00:15:31,081 --> 00:15:51,647
The mean of a cell is c transpose beta, for a vector c of 0s and 1s. Its estimate is c transpose beta hat. Its variance is c transpose, times vcov, times c. The confidence interval is the estimate, plus or minus 2.004 times the standard error.

63
00:15:52,097 --> 00:16:17,846
For students who find the pace fast and do not consider grad school, c is 1, 1, 0, 0. The estimate is 4.1852 minus 0.4709, which is 3.714. The standard error is 0.2684. The 95 percent confidence interval is 3.176 to 4.252.

64
00:16:18,296 --> 00:16:31,053
The other three cells use the same steps. The plot shows all four estimates and intervals. The lowest mean is for students who find the pace fast and consider grad school: 3.5.

65
00:16:31,503 --> 00:16:48,229
Now both covariates are continuous. The model has x i 1, x i 2, and their product. Fix x 2 at one value, for example 0. Then the mean is a straight line in x 1. The picture uses made-up numbers.

66
00:16:48,679 --> 00:17:03,868
Group the terms. The intercept is beta 0 plus beta 2 x 2. The slope is beta 1 plus beta 3 x 2. Both change when x 2 changes. The blue line is for x 2 equal to 1.

67
00:17:04,318 --> 00:17:18,953
Increase x 1 by one unit. The change in the mean is beta 1 plus beta 3 x 2. So the effect of x 1 depends on the value of x 2. The purple line is for x 2 equal to 2.

68
00:17:19,403 --> 00:17:31,264
So beta 1 is the change in mean y for a one unit increase in x 1, when x 2 is 0. This answers the first part of the exercise on slide 20.

69
00:17:31,714 --> 00:17:47,863
Practice question 2. Interpret beta 3 in this model. The hint says: write it as a difference of differences. Pause the video and try it. Let mu of x 1, x 2 be the mean of y. The table shows the mean at four points.

70
00:17:48,313 --> 00:17:59,300
In the row x 2 equals b, increase x 1 from a to a plus 1. From the last slide, the mean changes by beta 1 plus beta 3 times b.

71
00:17:59,750 --> 00:18:09,286
In the row x 2 equals b plus 1, the same increase changes the mean by beta 1, plus beta 3 times b plus 1.

72
00:18:09,736 --> 00:18:25,992
Subtract the two differences. The answer is beta 3. So beta 3 is how much the effect of a one unit increase in x 1 changes when x 2 increases by one unit. The same is true with x 1 and x 2 swapped.

73
00:18:26,442 --> 00:18:37,514
Sometimes a straight line does not fit the data well. These points follow a curve. The gray dashed line misses the trend. The picture uses made-up numbers.

74
00:18:37,964 --> 00:18:46,284
One way to make the model more flexible is to add a quadratic term, x i squared. The green curve is a parabola.

75
00:18:46,734 --> 00:18:59,875
The model is still a linear regression model, because it is linear in the betas. X gets one more column, x i squared. So beta hat, the t tests and the F tests all work as before.

76
00:19:00,325 --> 00:19:12,123
The tortoise data have 18 measurements. NumEggs is the number of eggs in the hatch of a tortoise. CarapDiam is the diameter of its carapace, the shell.

77
00:19:12,573 --> 00:19:16,242
The table shows the first six rows from slide 24.

78
00:19:16,692 --> 00:19:27,444
The plot shows all 18 points. The number of eggs goes up at first. Then it goes down for the largest tortoises. A straight line cannot follow this.

79
00:19:27,894 --> 00:19:50,571
Slide 25 fits the quadratic model. Here is its coefficient table. The rows are beta 0, beta 1, and beta 2. The estimates are minus 899.9, 5.857, and minus 0.009425. The letter e means a power of 10.

80
00:19:51,021 --> 00:20:13,165
Each t value is the estimate over its standard error. For the squared term, minus 0.009425 over 0.002829 is minus 3.332. The last column is the two-sided p-value of the t test of that coefficient equal to 0.

81
00:20:13,615 --> 00:20:24,815
The residual standard error is sigma hat, 2.671. Its degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1: 18 minus 3, which is 15.

82
00:20:25,265 --> 00:20:37,916
The first part of the output summarizes the residuals: the minimum, the first quartile, the median, the third quartile, and the maximum. The orange dots are the 18 residuals.

83
00:20:38,366 --> 00:20:42,163
Slide 26 shows the two fits. Here are the data.

84
00:20:42,613 --> 00:20:47,093
The gray dashed line is the straight line fit. It is almost flat.

85
00:20:47,543 --> 00:20:54,306
The green curve is the quadratic fit. It goes up and then down. It follows the data much better.

86
00:20:54,756 --> 00:21:07,855
Slide 27 asks: what is beta 1 here. In the quadratic model, the change in mean outcome for a one unit change in x varies with x. So no single number is the slope.

87
00:21:08,305 --> 00:21:24,987
As an aid, the slope of the curve at x is beta 1 plus 2 beta 2 x. At a diameter of 295, the slope is plus 0.296. In this region, larger tortoises have more eggs on average.

88
00:21:25,437 --> 00:21:40,008
At 325, the slope is minus 0.269. Here the mean goes down. The exact change from x to x plus 1 is beta 1, plus beta 2 times 2 x plus 1.

89
00:21:40,458 --> 00:21:59,381
The curve is highest where the slope is 0: at minus beta 1 hat over 2 beta 2 hat, which is 310.7. beta 1 alone is the slope at x equal to 0. That is far outside the data, so beta 1 alone has no useful meaning here.

90
00:21:59,831 --> 00:22:12,396
Slide 27 also asks how to test whether the quadratic model is more appropriate than the straight line. The two models differ only in the term beta 2 x i squared.

91
00:22:12,846 --> 00:22:24,323
So test the null hypothesis beta 2 equals 0 in the quadratic model. This is a t test with 18 minus 3, which is 15, degrees of freedom.

92
00:22:24,773 --> 00:22:46,405
From the table, t is minus 3.332. Its absolute value is larger than 2.131, the t quantile with 15 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 0.00455. We reject the null hypothesis. The quadratic model fits better.

93
00:22:46,855 --> 00:23:03,325
Beyond polynomial terms, linear regression can use any known functions of x. The model is the sum of beta j times f j of x i. Each f j is a known function, for example x, x squared, or log x.

94
00:23:03,775 --> 00:23:21,844
It is easy to add too many terms. Then the model can overfit. Overfit means that the curve follows the random noise in this sample, and not the true mean. The red curve uses all powers of x up to 7. It is an illustration computed from the data.

95
00:23:22,294 --> 00:23:30,337
Also, more terms make the coefficients harder to interpret. So there is a trade-off between fit and interpretability.

96
00:23:30,787 --> 00:23:52,163
Slide 29 gives the hierarchical principle. In general, fit hierarchically well-formulated models. This means: if the model has a higher-order term, it also has all the lower-order terms under it. If you include x 1 times x 2, also include the main effects x 1 and x 2.

97
00:23:52,613 --> 00:24:01,829
If you include the three-way product x 1 x 2 x 3, also include all three two-way products, and all three main effects.

98
00:24:02,279 --> 00:24:15,975
The same rule holds for polynomial terms. If you include x cubed, also include x squared and x. Without these terms, the model can have unexpected meanings. The next slide shows one.

99
00:24:16,425 --> 00:24:30,313
Here is a model that breaks the principle: beta 0 plus beta 2 x i squared, with no linear term. Its curve must have its lowest point at x equal to 0, if beta 2 is positive.

100
00:24:30,763 --> 00:24:41,301
Now shift x by a fixed amount b. For example, center x at its mean, so b is x bar. The blue curve is the same curve, moved by b.

101
00:24:41,751 --> 00:24:49,666
Expand the square. x i minus b, squared, is x i squared, minus 2 x i b, plus b squared.

102
00:24:50,116 --> 00:25:11,919
Group the terms. The model now has a linear term: minus 2 b beta 2, times x i. It appears only because of the shift. So the model without the x term is not the same model after a change of origin. A hierarchically well-formulated model, with x and x squared, does not have this problem.

103
00:25:12,369 --> 00:25:29,009
Practice question 1 uses the tortoise data. y is NumEggs and x is CarapDiam. Part a: fit Model A, the straight line, and test beta 1 equals 0 at the 5 percent level. Pause the video and try it.

104
00:25:29,459 --> 00:25:45,373
The fit, computed from the data: beta 1 hat is 0.0276, with a standard error of 0.0563. t is 0.490, with 16 degrees of freedom.

105
00:25:45,823 --> 00:26:03,786
The t quantile with 16 degrees of freedom is 2.120. The absolute value of t is smaller. The p-value is 0.631. We do not reject the null hypothesis. The straight line shows no linear association.

106
00:26:04,236 --> 00:26:18,337
Part b: fit Model B, the quadratic model, and test beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0. Pause the video and try it. This is a joint null, so use the F test with q equal to 2.

107
00:26:18,787 --> 00:26:44,814
F is beta star hat transpose, times V star inverse, times beta star hat, over q sigma hat squared. Computed from the data, F is 5.747. The 95 percent quantile of F with 2 and 15 degrees of freedom is 3.682. The p-value is 0.0140. We reject the null hypothesis.

108
00:26:45,264 --> 00:27:00,005
This does not conflict with part a. Part a looks only for a straight-line trend. The mean goes up and then down, so the best straight line is almost flat. Model B can follow the curve, so it finds the association.

109
00:27:00,455 --> 00:27:08,157
Part c: fit Model C, which adds x cubed, and test beta 3 equals 0. Pause the video and try it.

110
00:27:08,607 --> 00:27:26,847
Computed from the data, t for beta 3 hat is 0.613, with 14 degrees of freedom. The t quantile is 2.145. The p-value is 0.550. We do not reject the null hypothesis.

111
00:27:27,297 --> 00:27:38,539
The purple dashed cubic curve is almost on top of the green quadratic curve. The cubic term adds little. So Model C is not more appropriate than Model B.

112
00:27:38,989 --> 00:28:05,869
Part d: in Model C, test beta 2 equals beta 3 equals 0. Pause the video and try it. Use the F test with q equal to 2 and 14 degrees of freedom. Computed from the data, F is 5.508. The 95 percent quantile is 3.739. The p-value is 0.0172. We reject the null hypothesis.

113
00:28:06,319 --> 00:28:27,354
This does not conflict with part c. Part c asks if x cubed helps when x squared is already in the model. Part d asks if x squared and x cubed together help, beyond x. Also, each single t value in Model C is small: all are below 0.75 in absolute value.

114
00:28:27,804 --> 00:28:50,716
The plot shows the reason. On the range 284 to 334, x squared and x cubed lie almost on a straight line. Their correlation is 0.9997. So they carry almost the same information. One of them alone adds little when the other is in the model. A later lecture studies this problem.

115
00:28:51,166 --> 00:29:00,318
Part e: fit Model D, with x and x cubed, but no x squared. Test beta 2 equals 0. Pause the video and try it.

116
00:29:00,768 --> 00:29:27,797
In Model D, beta 2 is the coefficient of x cubed. Computed from the data, t is minus 3.317, with 15 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 0.0047. We reject the null hypothesis. Model D fits almost as well as Model B. The teal curve is close to the green curve, and sigma hat is 2.676, against 2.671.

117
00:29:28,247 --> 00:29:35,799
But Model D has x cubed and not x squared. So it breaks the hierarchical principle. Prefer Model B.

118
00:29:36,249 --> 00:29:47,343
Practice question 3. Use the real estate data. The model has SqrFeet, Bedrooms, and their product. Interpret the results. Pause the video and try it.

119
00:29:47,793 --> 00:30:02,875
The table is computed from the data. beta 0 hat is minus 209.3. It is the mean price at 0 square feet and 0 bedrooms. No such house exists, so it has no practical meaning.

120
00:30:03,325 --> 00:30:23,699
beta 1 hat is 0.2269. For a house with 0 bedrooms, the mean price goes up by 0.2269 thousand dollars per square foot. beta 2 hat is 30.22. It is the change in mean price for one more bedroom, at 0 square feet.

121
00:30:24,149 --> 00:30:46,698
beta 3 hat is minus 0.0159. Each extra bedroom lowers the slope per square foot by 0.0159 thousand dollars. So for houses with more bedrooms, one more square foot adds less to the mean price. Its t value is large in absolute value: minus 4.11.

122
00:30:47,148 --> 00:31:01,377
Now plot the fitted line for a given number of bedrooms. Put the number of bedrooms, k, into the model. The intercept is beta 0 plus beta 2 k. The slope is beta 1 plus beta 3 k.

123
00:31:01,827 --> 00:31:18,382
0 bedrooms: the intercept is minus 209.26, and the slope is 0.2269. No house in the data has 0 bedrooms. So this line is an extrapolation: it goes outside the data.

124
00:31:18,832 --> 00:31:30,075
1 bedroom: the intercept is minus 179.04, and the slope is 0.2110. The line is a little flatter.

125
00:31:30,525 --> 00:31:50,834
7 bedrooms: the intercept is 2.29, and the slope is 0.1155. This line is much flatter. Only 3 houses have 7 bedrooms, all with 2812 square feet or more. So the lines cross: the interaction changes the slope.

126
00:31:51,284 --> 00:32:08,756
To recap. An interaction term is a product of two covariates. With indicator times weight, each group gets its own intercept and its own slope. beta 1 M W is the MW slope minus the N slope: a difference in differences.

